          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Corten steel need to be painted or coated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No, Corten is specifically designed to develop its own protective oxide layer through natural weathering, which is the opposite approach to standard steel that requires an applied protective coating to resist corrosion."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does it take for Corten to reach its final colour?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The protective rust orange layer typically stabilises within one to two years of weather exposure, depending on local climate and rainfall, after which the oxidation rate slows down significantly and the colour stays relatively stable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Corten stain nearby surfaces during weathering?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can during the initial weathering period if drainage isn't detailed correctly, since rainwater carrying rust particles can mark adjacent light-coloured materials. Proper drainage planning at the design stage genuinely prevents this from becoming a problem."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Corten suitable for coastal buildings in India?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Corten can be used in coastal environments, but its weathering behaviour is less predictable in heavy salt air than in drier climates, and it's worth discussing this specifically with your facade designer for genuinely coastal projects before committing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Corten more expensive than standard aluminium?",
